{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the workspace head lineage repair and diagnosis at head 7d74234c21ec76e8c7f2fa8c60f50b8dad05d3c3, tree ce3ad078, for request 4f3336c1 as amended by ruling 90ee0518, and review request a9f30baa. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds seven commits to main 23865f98 and changes 14 paths. I read the note first, then all four production diffs and the rules they rely on, and reviewed the reader as an authority predicate under section 7 of `docs/kernel.md`. I ran no independent second read.\n\n## The predicate against ruling 90ee0518\n\n1. The source matches the real rule. `register.ts:412-431` sends `create-directory` from the selected outcome and names the opening `found` entry in `fields.claim`. The old reader required one fact to be both. I confirm the mismatch by reading the source. No record shows that it caused either S1 stop, and the note says so.\n2. The outcome is bound. The reader requires a Register outcome with the installed pin as owner, kind `create-repository`, result `confirmed` and basis `own-answer`. It requires the actual send to the Directory seed and exactly one attempt effect for that operation and attempt, confirmed and selected. A late confirmed outcome carries `selected: false`, so it can't pass even with a borrowed send.\n3. The claim is bound separately.\n   - `fields.claim` must sit in the same Register incarnation, at the operation's opening position, before the outcome.\n   - `proofEntry` compares the entry with the fact's hash, position and scope.\n   - The act must be a signed `found` addressed to the installed Register, with exactly one operation effect at the ordinal, the same owner and kind, and an attempt within its bound.\n4. The older checks stay. The seed effect still derives the Directory's scope, and the Destination checks are unchanged apart from comparing `fields.claim` with the claim fact. `isOperationId` admits only `seq:k`, so the numeric parse is safe.\n5. The bound rises by one read. The sequence is Directory, lane source, outcome, Register, claim and Destination. The five-second deadline and the one-MiB proof bound don't change.\n6. The real port can serve the new read. `namespace.ts` reaches `object.ts:725` `proofSource`, which reads by exact fact and has no filter on entry kind. A positive result on that real transport is still unobserved.\n7. The audit holds. `workspaceRepository` has one production caller, at `workspace-host.ts:86`. `host-wiring.ts:26` and `archive-host-item.ts:31` already read the claim separately from the source. A host-none room has no create-repository outcome, so the reader refuses it, and that room has no repository for a workspace to use.\n\n## The witness\n\n- The fixture judges Register17, Directory16 and Destination12 through the maintained rules, including an unknown first attempt and a late unselected confirmation. Lane creation, proof transport and the provider are labelled stand-ins.\n- Both refusals are present, with the read count that shows which guard decided. The forged claim stops after four reads, and so does the unselected outcome with a borrowed send.\n- The retained comparison fails three assertions on the old reader, and the note correctly says that it combines the predicate with the five-read bound. I checked the receipt digests and the note's account. I did not rerun the controls.\n\n## Diagnosis\n\n- A report holds fixed words, an allowlisted error name and an optional request ID, and it never reads a message. `diag.ts` shows this, and the tests check that the private text stays out of both sinks.\n- Return and throw behavior holds. The Artifacts read keeps its revocation in `finally`, a revocation failure doesn't replace the read's outcome, and the outer catch rethrows the same failure. WorkspaceHost still returns null.\n- The eight stages are in `STEPS`, and `workspace-lineage-unavailable` is in `EVENTS`. The metric place stays inside a closed list.\n\n## Gate and checks at this head\n\n- The full gate at c4de6a52, tree 8a598235, closed 0/0 with all stages passing. Its `test.log` shows Node 84, Counting 56, Plain 3,193 with one skip and Native 888 with two skips.\n- From c4de6a52 to this head, only the note and the manifest change, so the documents-only rule applies and no new gate is due.\n- The evidence verifies. The manifest has SHA-256 ab27d06e. All 192 of its rows that carry a path and digest match. The focused collection has 303 files, the gate collection has 280, each `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean, and no file is writable. The receipts for the failed security and type checks stay at their own checkpoints.\n- The 14 published artifacts match the 14 changed paths.\n- I ran these once each:\n  - `workspace-lineage.test.ts` passes 8 of 8;\n  - `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 of 10;\n  - `check-kernel-boundary` checks 127 sources with 0 refusals;\n  - `check-notes` gives 0 warnings;\n  - `check-commit-messages` checks 7 with 0 flagged;\n  - `check-doc-links` on an export finds 774 links with 0 broken;\n  - `git diff --check` is clean.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 23865f98 gives tree ce3ad078. The worktree stayed clean.\n- Plan 034 and its index say that the recovery reader is not scheduled, as ruling item 4 asks.\n\n## For the planner: what this repair changes after deployment\n\n1. The repair opens provider paths on real rooms. By the same source reading, the old reader returned null on any room created through the real Register. So fork, stage, check, mint, revoke and deletion all depended on a lineage that could not succeed there, as head did. This head makes all of them reachable, and only a stand-in provider has tested them through this ancestry. The J4 successor packet and the third S1 room are the first live runs. Their reports should say that.\n2. A lineage refusal won't name its guard. The event `workspace-lineage-unavailable` says that the reader returned null, and it doesn't say which predicate refused. If the third S1 room stops there, the diagnosis ends at that word, and the next step needs the room's real entries. This meets the fixed-word rule, and I don't ask for more detail in this head.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. The fixture covers Register17 only. Later Registers use the same `create-directory` rule, and `DIRECTORY_OF` selects the family exactly, so I expect no difference. No test shows it.\n2. `workspace-lineage-read` and `workspace-lineage` report for every WorkspaceHost kind, while `workspace-head-send` reports for head only. A failure in another kind that happens outside the reader stays silent, as it did before. The request asked for the head path.\n3. Every failed Artifacts read now reports, on all six `#read` callers. Reads are bounded by their operations, so I don't expect a volume problem. The planner can watch it after deployment.\n4. The controls keep outer streams, not the helper's internal JSON reports. The note says so.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe reader stays a host check of existing creation facts. It adds no kernel export, scope kind, capability, record, pin or outcome meaning. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nThe predicate binds the outcome, the send, the selection and the claim as separate facts, and each is compared with its hash. It adds no fallback, and no caller-supplied repository or current summary enters. Diagnoses hold no provider text. Approved, with point 1 for the planner's deployment.\n\n## Simplification\n\nOne reader changes, the three scripted factories give way to one fixture built on real rules, and the diagnosis uses the existing sink. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: the repair, the real-rule witness, both refusals, the audit, the plan status, the gate and the evidence all hold.\n\nThis approval covers local source only. It claims no hosted or provider success and no cause for the S1 stops. It releases no J4 or S1 action.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2c0d86ad395afaddbb9a0c4fc04823c804a111ea","binding":"assigned","head":"7d74234c21ec76e8c7f2fa8c60f50b8dad05d3c3","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6556eb9f98773a87c256fa217bd2d00ebfbab493\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d38ace314be6c19159fa73b49a3c7755d49ac492\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6556eb9f98773a87c256fa217bd2d00ebfbab493","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:222550aa5a88fd70f299d31beafb99859ebc1b39, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2c0d86ad395afaddbb9a0c4fc04823c804a111ea, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3a227b675e006343ba452eefa84b05f6d461b588, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3a3ac7659402f96f64b90b63412845198e6cc1b5, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5f508238abdd6ca159f71f55e2873986bb2258b9, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6556eb9f98773a87c256fa217bd2d00ebfbab493, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:92568e77dddfda52fc9a08e92b287bc92b519fdd, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:954df1c19964c3aeefdfe282af92c186996e485c, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9c5696673ca3fb0bfe6738ba231726732ea0124b, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a9f30baaba1c4b09982f522dd88b0bed50adb8df, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b37e5c0037007d1f9833bf5bfa7bee44e460d74a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c360235b10af48e46da9946a4b72efe7e8ab670a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c7cd2cfa3366a0ffd0b5de76db18776df004a179, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f202420a7d